Task 1: Filtering Data with WHERE 
Find students above age 20 
SELECT *  
FROM Students 
WHERE Age > 20; 
Filter courses taught by a specific instructor 
SELECT * 
FROM Courses 
WHERE Instructor = 'Dr. Sharma'; 
Task 2: Sorting with ORDER BY 
Sort students alphabetically 
SELECT * 
FROM Students 
ORDER BY StudentName ASC; 
Sort courses by credits (highest first) 
SELECT * 
FROM Courses 
ORDER BY Credits DESC; 
Task 3: Aggregation with GROUP BY 
Count number of students per course 
SELECT CourseID, COUNT(StudentID) AS Student_Count 
FROM Students 
GROUP BY CourseID; 
Find average age of students 
SELECT AVG(Age) AS Average_Age 
FROM Students; 
Task 4: Nested Subqueries 
Find students enrolled in courses with more than 3 credits 
SELECT StudentName 
FROM Students 
WHERE CourseID IN ( 
SELECT CourseID 
FROM Courses 
WHERE Credits > 3 
); 
Conclusion 
In this experiment, advanced SQL querying techniques such as filtering using WHERE, sorting 
using ORDER BY, aggregation using GROUP BY, and nested subqueries were implemented in a 
Student Course Management System. These operations allow efficient retrieval and analysis of 
database records. The experiment demonstrates how SQL can be used to manage and analyze 
educational data effectively. 
